<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viljandi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fellin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Viljandi is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, on a lake in the old land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>. Its hillfort was a chief stronghold of the Sakalians until German crusaders captured it in the 1210s and finally in 1224, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> built Fellin castle there, one of the largest castles of the order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. The town later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viljandi.jpg" alt="Viljandi" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vaido Otsar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viljandi_raekoda_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Viljandi lies on the north-western shore of Lake Viljandi, in a primeval valley, between the Estonian cities of Pärnu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a>. It is the capital of Viljandi County. Its symbol is a white rose, and 720 white roses were planted before the city hall for the anniversary in 2003. The first records of settlement near the town date to the fifth millennium BCE, and the earthen stronghold is first mentioned in 1154 in the commentaries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a>'s world atlas Geography.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the twelfth century a permanent settlement emerged around the stronghold, which became the economic centre of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> district. In 1211 the hillfort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> was besieged by a joint army of Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>. The Livonian Sword Brethren, later the Livonian Order, started a powerful Order centre in place of the wooden stronghold in 1224, and the following year Grand Master Volquin led the building of Viljandi Castle on the site of the hillfort. It was one of the largest castles of the Baltic region, a commander was appointed to it from 1248, and it was rebuilt and modernised over the next two hundred years.</p><p>In the thirteenth century a medieval town arose on the north side of the stronghold, with the Hamburg-Riga town bylaws, first recorded in 1283. In the first half of the fourteenth century Viljandi joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, as an important stop for merchants travelling to and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1481 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of Russia besieged the castle but could not take it. The decline began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, and in 1560 the forces of Knyaz Andrey Kurbsky of Muscovite Russia seized and demolished the town and the stronghold. They were destroyed again in the Polish–Russian War of the early seventeenth century, and under Swedish rule the town bylaws were cancelled. They were restored in 1783, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>'s reforms made Viljandi a district town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Viljandi has about 17,000 inhabitants. The ruins of the castle lie in a park, and the town belongs to the international city league the Hanse. It is sometimes called the cultural capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, partly because of the Viljandi Culture Academy, and the Viljandi Folk Music Festival is held each summer. The newspaper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> was founded there in 1878, and the railway station opened in 1897.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viljandi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near <b>Viljandi</b>, the Livonian Brothers of the Sword and their Liv and Latgalian allies defeated an Estonian army of 6,000 under Lembitu of Sakala.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Order's knight-brothers, mostly from Saxony and Westphalia, lived under a monastic rule and governed conquered land from castles such as Wenden (Cēsis) and <b>Fellin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a></li><li><blockquote>Sakala, in south-western central Estonia, was one of the major ancient Estonian lands, with hillforts at <b>Viljandi</b> and Lehola.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a></li></ul></section>
